/*Try this out:
- ctrl+f7 toggles the status bar below for general info
- ctrl+alt+d for debug mode
- ctrl+alt+c to compile
- put a breakpoint in left grey column with mouse left click
or f9 sets a breakpoint at the text cursor line
- run the code using f5 and step over each instruction with f10
- ctrl+shift+g to move from code to asm lines
*/
main :: () {
	/*Arrays are a sequence of values in memory.

	General array view form is :
	array_name : [] array_type;

	Arrays can be stored on the stack or heap.
	To store an array on the heap :
	*/	
	ha : [] int = NewArray(3, int); //This creates 3 integers on the heap.

	/*Put ha in the top-right watch window and press enter or 
	click drop(array) to view the array contents during execution.

	To change the count put "ha,2" in the watch window.

	Each value can be accessed through ha[x].
	*/
	ha[0] = 1;
	ha[1] = 2;
	ha[2] = ha[1];

	/*An array is a struct with 2 values
	Array :: struct {
		count : int;
		data : *data_type;
	}

	An array view stores the above struct on the stack.
	"count" is how many members are in the array.
	"data" is the address where the data of the array starts.
	Try putting these in the top-right watch window.
	*/
	ha_count : int = ha.count;
	ha_data : *int = ha.data;

	/*To learn about structs
	load_op structs guides/programming/structs
	*/

	/*Array access is an add and multiplication from the array data address.
	if ha.data is at address byte "d" then
	ha[0] = [d + 0 * 8] where 8 bytes is the size of each value since this is an integer array
	ha[1] = [d + 1 * 8]
	ha[2] = [d + 2 * 8]

	General array access is
	ha[x] = [ha.data + x * size_of(data_type)]

	ctrl+shift+g will move to the corresponding asm code
	setting a breakpoint here will show all instructions for this array access
	*/
	ha[2] = 3;

	//size_of is the size in bytes of any data type and is known at compile-time.
	i_size : int = size_of(int);

	/*Addresses and arithmetic can be used directly to do array access.
	The following to lines are the same
	*/
	fa_2_address : *int = *ha[2];
	fa_2_data : *int = ha.data + 2; //note that 2 is implicitly multiplied by 8 here because size_of(int) is 8 bytes

	//The following 2 lines will both access the same data address and so will return the same data value.
	fa_2_av : int = fa_2_address.*;
	fa_2_dv : int = fa_2_data.*;


	/*General fixed array view form is
	array_name : [array_count] array_type;

	A fixed array stores its values directly in memory on the stack
	and so the count and data member values are known only at compile time and 
	not stored in memory and therefore not known at runtime.

	To store an array on the stack :
	*/
	fa : [2] int; //Only 2 integers on the stack.

	//To view the data address of fa, put "*fa" in the top-right watch window.
	fa_count : int = fa.count; //this is set in the instruction directly at compile-time
	fa_data : *int = fa.data; //this is fixed on the stack at compile-time

	//Array access for fixed arrays is generally the same as array views.
	fa[0] = 5;
	fa[1] = 10;

	//Array literals are fixed arrays which can be set directly on declaration
	al : [3] int = int.[0,4,7];
	al2 := int.[0,4,7]; //we can omit the type here since it is inferred

	//Casting a fixed array to an array view is generally not allowed.
	//An exception is that we can declare a fixed array as an array view.
	hl : [] int = al;
}